  "full_response": "Rate limiting is a technique used to control the amount of incoming or outgoing traffic to or from a server, application, or network. It is primarily used to prevent abuse, ensure fair usage, manage resources, maintain performance, and protect against DoS (Denial of Service) attacks. By limiting the number of requests a user, client, or IP address can make within a specified time frame, rate limiting ensures that the service remains available and responsive to all users.\n\nCommon algorithms used to implement rate limiting include:\n\n1. **Token Bucket Algorithm**:\n   - In this algorithm, tokens are added to a bucket at a regular rate. Each incoming request requires a token to proceed. If the bucket is empty, the request is denied until a token becomes available. This algorithm is flexible as it can handle varying burst rates of requests while enforcing a steady rate limit.\n\n2. **Leaky Bucket Algorithm**:\n   - This algorithm models the flow of requests like water in a leaky bucket; the bucket has a fixed capacity, and requests fill the bucket at a steady rate. If the bucket overflows (i.e., becomes full), new requests are either queued or dropped. It provides a consistent output rate for requests irrespective of the burst input rate.\n\n3. **Fixed Window Counter**:\n   - In this simple implementation, a counter is reset at regular intervals (windows). Each request increments the counter, and requests are denied once the counter exceeds a predefined limit. This method is easy to implement but can allow a burst in traffic at the start of each window.\n\n4. **Sliding Window Log**:\n   - This algorithm keeps track of each request timestamp in a log, which can be checked to ensure the number of requests in any given window (the current interval plus the previous one) does not exceed the limit. It offers a more accurate enforcement than a fixed window but at the cost of increased resource usage due to logging.\n\n5. **Sliding Window Counter**:\n   - This is a hybrid approach that aims to combine the simplicity of fixed window counters with more flexibility by dividing time into smaller sub-windows. The rate limit check is more distributed over time, reducing bursts at the window boundaries.\n\n6. **Revolving Window Algorithm**:\n   - A variation of the sliding window that maintains counters and timestamps for several small intervals (buckets), effectively spreading the traffic checks across these finer intervals for a smoother and more precise limit enforcement.\n\nEach of these algorithms has its advantages and trade-offs in terms of complexity, precision, and resource consumption, so the",
